- **`[stakeholder]`** (usually a named human, not a scratch dir) — owns the spec and backlog
  priority; the default authority for ambiguous, irreversible, or consequential decisions; gives
  go/no-go at each review checkpoint below. Named individual: [name/contact].
- **`[orchestrator]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — coordinates the team, enforces gates, is the point of
  contact with the stakeholder.
- **`[planner]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — turns the spec into a slice plan with acceptance criteria,
  dependency order, risk register, open-questions log. Output: `[roadmap/plan doc path]`.
- **`[architect]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — owns cross-cutting decisions, contracts, data model, test
  strategy, performance/cost trade-offs, and revising prior ADRs. Output: `[architecture doc
  path]`, ADRs in `[adr dir]`.
- **`[designer]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — [if this project has a user-facing surface: owns the
  design system and accessibility standard, and the cost/benefit call on design asks. Output:
  `[design system doc path]`. **If this project has no user-facing surface, delete this row and
  say so explicitly rather than leaving it unfilled** — see
  trellis/principles/02-roles-and-team-model.md#designer.]
- **`[implementer]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — writes tests first, then code, one slice at a time.
- **`[validator]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — independent of the implementer; verifies against the
  spec with tests it writes itself (never reads or relies on the implementer's tests), adds
  edge/regression tests.
- **`[reviewer]`** (`[scratch dir]`) — read-only; quality, standards, docs, boundaries, and (if
  applicable) confirms the designer's check happened; verdict only, does not edit.
- **`[deployer/operator]`** — the only role permitted to reach real production systems, and only
  with [per-call human approval / whatever your project's rule is]. Not part of the build loop.
  Owns running this project's observability and rollback approach (below) for real.

Durable knowledge discovered by any role is promoted out of scratch into permanent project or
process knowledge before the slice closes — see `[knowledge-promotion workflow path]`.

## Workflow

Every "stop for review" below is a stop for the stakeholder named above, decided once as a
cadence during setup — not renegotiated slice by slice (see
trellis/principles/03-workflow-lifecycle.md).

**Phase 0 — Scaffolding.** [What this means concretely for this project.] Stop for stakeholder
review.

**Phase 1 — Plan.** Delegate to `[planner]`. Stop for stakeholder review.

**Phase 2 — Architect.** Delegate to `[architect]` (and `[designer]`, if applicable, for
design/accessibility decisions). Stop for stakeholder review.

**Phase 3…N — Implement, one slice at a time.** Run `[slice-implementation-loop workflow path]`
and do not start the next slice until the current one closes — except independent slices
explicitly approved for parallel work (see "Parallel work" below).

**Final phase.** End-to-end validation against the spec; Definition of Done checklist
(`[definition-of-done doc path]`); [operator smoke-test checklist if applicable]; final summary
for the stakeholder.

